  <div class="info-section">
    <h2>Why Accurate Computational Tools Matter in Technology</h2>
    <p>In both computer hardware architecture and electrical engineering, small discrepancies in load matching produce compounding inefficiencies. A mismatch between a high-end graphics card and an entry-level CPU can waste hundreds of dollars in unutilized silicon capacity, while an undersized wire gauge across a long distance causes severe voltage drop, excessive heat buildup, and electrical fire hazards.</p>

    <h3>Understanding Bottlenecks in PC Gaming &amp; Workstations</h3>
    <p>A hardware bottleneck occurs when one component operates at maximum capacity while other connected components are forced to idle, capping overall system performance. In modern PC gaming:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>CPU Bottleneck (Common at 1080p):</strong> At lower resolutions, the graphics card renders frames rapidly, forcing the CPU to prepare draw calls at blistering speeds. If the CPU cannot keep pace, you experience micro-stutters and frame rate dips.</li>
      <li><strong>GPU Bottleneck (Common at 1440p &amp; 4K):</strong> At higher resolutions, pixel rendering demands increase exponentially. Having a 95% to 99% GPU utilization during gaming is ideal, as it indicates you are fully exploiting your graphics card investment.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>National Electrical Code (NEC) Voltage Drop Rules</h3>
    <p>The National Electrical Code (NEC Article 210.19(A) Informational Note 4) recommends that the maximum voltage drop on branch circuits should not exceed <strong>3%</strong>, and total combined voltage drop on both feeder and branch conductors should not exceed <strong>5%</strong>. Exceeding these thresholds causes motors to overheat, sensitive digital electronics to reset, and lights to visibly flicker.</p>
  </div>

    <div class="info-section">
    <h2>Best Practices for Hardware &amp; Electrical Sizing</h2>
    <p>Before making hardware purchases or running conduit for long distance electrical lines, consider these critical planning factors:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Target Balanced Hardware Upgrades:</strong> When upgrading to a high-end GPU (like an RTX 4080 or RX 7900 XTX), verify that your CPU possesses enough multi-threaded processing power to support background OS tasks, streaming, and Discord without throttling frame times.</li>
      <li><strong>Thermal Throttling Checks:</strong> A high bottleneck score can sometimes be caused by thermal throttling rather than silicon limitations. Ensure adequate case airflow and CPU cooler sizing.</li>
      <li><strong>Upsizing Conductors for Long Runs:</strong> When branch circuits exceed 100 feet in length, standard wire gauge (14 AWG for 15A or 12 AWG for 20A) frequently produces unacceptable voltage drop. Stepping up one wire gauge (e.g., from 12 AWG to 10 AWG) eliminates line resistance and maintains full operating voltage for power tools and appliances.</li>
      <li><strong>Ambient Temperature De-Rating:</strong> Conductors routed through attics or exterior conduit exposed to direct sunlight require NEC ampacity adjustment factors to prevent insulation degradation.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="expert-note">
    <strong>Editorial Standards:</strong> All technology calculation models on AmericaCalculator.com are calibrated against manufacturer technical documentation (including Intel, AMD, and NVIDIA hardware specifications) and NFPA 70 National Electrical Code tables. Updated for <%= currentYear %>.
  </div>
